<br />I enjoyed this book &amp; its characters (main &amp; supporting), even more than Filthy Beginnings #4A - the start of this storyline. I definitely want to read more from this author in the future. But... there are a few things that I found could have been handled slightly differently - it's probably just me nit picking thou.<br />The intimacy is very drawn-out (similar to Filthy Beginnings) for around half the book. There are just moments caught between high activity. This eventually became slightly repetitive. <br />Fortunately MC's do eventually reveal their personal thoughts, feelings &amp; reasoning with each other BEFORE the last chapter, which is great, otherwise it can be extremely frustrating for the reader, who knows they just need to be honest with each other. <br />Weirdly, non-heterosexual sexuality isn't something even mentioned in this universe (until second half of book), either/neither bisexual, homosexual, asexual or any other sexuality. Which is strange, given: the futuristic nature of the story; that other species can/do interact regularly; and that interspecies procreation can also be possible &amp; fairly common - you'd expect different preferences and anatomies within such a diverse advanced society, would breed acceptance that, nothing is "normal".<br />There's also the question of why the female MC (Scarlett) would even think that the person she cares about (ie male MC Damien) could be ignorant/intolerant. If she was so unsure of a love interests opinion, on an important topic, couldn't/wouldn't she of, broached the subject in a nonspecific way, to gleen their views/prejudices etc earlier.<br />I was confused as to why the "unspoken" topic isn't/wasn't in any other area of the story. You don't hear/see any indications of intolerance elsewhere in the narrative, its not in any of the different characters (eg no slurs, "jokes" etc are spoken), or generally in their society (arrests, rules stated somewhere or by someone etc). It wasn’t once specifically referenced until the last stages of the book.<br />But I'm probably diving too deep into a work of romantic fiction.<br />I do really like the storyline, &amp; all but 1 (because of their above question avoidance &amp; assumptions - unnecessary martyrdom annoys me) of the "good guy" characters (the "bad" ones didn't have any redeeming features). I enjoyed (&amp; appreciated) all the characters' realistic thoughts/actions/dialogue, etc. And there are a couple of things I didn't see coming at all, total surprises. Finally, I think the title is a misnomer. Nothing in this book was 'filthy' or 'royal'. Male MC continuously describing the relationship as 'filthy' was incongruous.</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section><div class="Alert Alert--informational">This entire review has been hidden because of spoilers.<div class="Alert__footer"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium" aria-label="Activate this button to show the hidden review."><span class="Button__labelItem">Show full review</span></button></div></div></div></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"></span><span tabindex="-1"></span></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="actions"><div class="SocialFooter__action"><div class="Button__container"><button type="button" class="Button Button--inline Button--medium" aria-label="Review unliked. Press to like review"><span class="Button__labelItem"><i class="Icon LikeIcon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M14.4813282,6.41129383 L14.586127,6.97676447 C14.5964421,7.03541935 14.6071117,7.09675198 14.6181364,7.1607667 L14.8551301,8.60363442 L17.440983,8.60363442 C18.1623576,8.60363442 18.8218187,9.01120377 19.1444272,9.65642083 L19.1708204,9.70920723 C19.4523479,10.2722622 19.4523479,10.9350066 19.1708204,11.4980616 L19.118034,11.6036344 L19.1708204,11.7092072 C19.4523479,12.2722622 19.4523479,12.9350066 19.1708204,13.4980